  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000124042,
      "SummaryText": "Georgia Power Company operates Plant Scherer on behalf of itself and its co-owners. The generating units at the facility utilize anhydrous ammonia and selective catalytic reduction (SCR) to control nitrogen oxide (NOx) emissions. Chlorine is also used in cooling towers, general service water, and water treatment facilities for water chlorination. The ammonia system and systems utilizing chlorine are RMP-covered processes because both chemicals are stored in quantities above their threshold quantities. The facility is also regulated by the Process Safety Management (PSM) program; therefore, the covered RMP processes are Program Level 3. There have not been any accidental releases of anhydrous ammonia or chlorine in the 5-year history preceding this submittal.  \n\nPlant Scherer is a responding facility and maintains an emergency response team (ERT) onsite to respond to an accidental release. The facility maintains an emergency action plan (EAP) which details emergency response procedures. The facility routinely performs required drills to ensure a prompt response during emergency situations. Process control and mitigation systems, including berms and fogging systems, have been installed to prevent and mitigate releases. In addition, the facility has taken steps such as implementing procedures and training programs to ensure personnel are equipped to safely and consistently handle RMP chemicals as a means of preventing accidental releases. \n\nCurrently, there are not any planned changes to improve safety related to the covered process. Potential changes are continuously evaluated and implemented through the PSM audit program, which requires audits on a three-year cycle. \n\nAs required by EPA regulations, this RMP contains a worst-case release scenario release from the facility and at least one alternative release scenario for the release of each RMP chemical. EPA's April 1999 Guidance for Off-site Release Analysis states that since worst case scenario distances are based"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000124042,
      "SummaryText": "on modeling conditions that are unlikely to occur, and since modeling of any scenario that results in large distances is very uncertain, EPA strongly urges communities and industry not to rely on the results of worst case modeling or any modeling that result in very large toxic end point distances in emergency planning and response. Results of alternative models for Plant Scherer show that an accidental release of anhydrous ammonia will not leave the plant site and will not affect any public receptors."
    }
  ]
